.Dd September 3, 2026
.Dt LOCALCRYPTO 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm localcrypto
.Nd capability-descriptor cryptography broker
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
daemon is the capability system's cryptography broker.
It is a socket-free capability-plane service: it exposes the well-known name
.Dq Li system.Crypto
and serves each client on its own
.Xr mac_capability 4
channel.
It mints kernel-backed crypto session
.Em descriptors
on request.
The actual cryptographic work runs through the returned
.Dv DTYPE_CRYPTO
descriptor, so no plaintext key material is ever materialized in userspace:
keys are minted inside the kernel behind the descriptor, and
.Nm
scrubs any public outputs with
.Xr explicit_bzero 3
before replying.
.Pp
.Nm
is launched and supervised by
.Xr serviced 8
from the
.Pa /Capabilities/System/Crypto.cap
bundle; it is not started by
.Xr init 8 ,
and it is not intended to be run from the command line.
.Pp
The kernel side is provided by the
.Xr crypto 4
.Pq Pa /dev/crypto
device from the
.Sy cryptodev
module.
Before becoming a provider,
.Nm
self-serves that module by name through
.Dq Li system.SystemExtension
.Pq Xr sysextd 8 ,
so it does not rely on PID 1 or
.Xr serviced 8
to load it.
.Ss Operations
A request names one operation over the client channel:
.Bl -tag -width ".Cm GENERATE_KEY"
.It Cm GENERATE
Mint a symmetric or AEAD cipher and/or HMAC session and return its descriptor.
.It Cm GENERATE_KEY
Mint an asymmetric keypair
.Pq X25519 for key exchange, or Ed25519 for signing
and return its descriptor together with the public key.
.It Cm CREATE
Create an owner-scoped named persistent key.
.It Cm LEASE
Return a session descriptor for an existing named key.
.It Cm ROTATE
Advance a named key to a new generation.
.It Cm DELETE
Remove a named key.
.El
.Pp
Named keys are
.Em owner-scoped
by the caller's unforgeable channel label: one label can never name, lease,
rotate, or delete another label's keys.
.Ss Parameter policy
All request parameters are validated server-side before any descriptor is
minted.
The policy enforces cipher and MAC key lengths, exact IV and tag lengths per
algorithm, AEAD cipher and MAC pairing, allowed capability rights per
algorithm, an optional NIST-approved-profile gate, a bounded time-to-live, and
a restricted character set for named-key names.
An out-of-policy request is rejected without reaching the kernel.
.Ss Worker isolation
The long-lived accept parent hands each accepted client to its own
.Xr pdfork 2 Ns 'd
worker.
A worker adopts the client channel, enters capability mode with
.Xr cap_enter 2 ,
and drops inherited authority.
Its only retained kernel handle is the
.Pa /dev/crypto
control descriptor, which is limited to
.Dv CAP_IOCTL
plus a six-command ioctl allowlist covering exactly the
descriptor-minting operations above; it can do nothing else.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".Pa /Capabilities/System/Crypto.cap" -compact
.It Pa /dev/crypto
kernel crypto control device
.It Pa /Capabilities/System/Crypto.cap
capability bundle shipping
.Nm
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cap_enter 2 ,
.Xr pdfork 2 ,
.Xr explicit_bzero 3 ,
.Xr crypto 4 ,
.Xr mac_capability 4 ,
.Xr serviced 8 ,
.Xr sysextd 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
daemon appeared in
.Fx 5BSD
as the capability-descriptor cryptography broker.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Kory Heard
